## Why This Is Asked

Tests low-level memory management, determinism, and fragmentation handling in tight constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size pool vs variable allocations
- In-block free-list approach
- Alignment and fragmentation
- Initialization, allocation, freeing, and testing

## Code Example

```c
/* Initialization and simple allocator skeleton */
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; } Block;

static uint8_t pool[4096];
static Block* free_list;

void pool_init(void){
  free_list = (Block*)pool;
  Block* cur = free_list;
  for (size_t i=0; i< (4096/32)-1; ++i){
    cur->next = (Block*)((uint8_t*)cur + 32);
    cur = cur->next;
  }
  cur->next = NULL;
}
void* pool_alloc(size_t s){
  if (s>32) return NULL;
  if (!free_list) return NULL;
  Block* b = free_list;
  free_list = b->next;
  return (void*)b;
}
void pool_free(void* p){
  ((Block*)p)->next = free_list;
  free_list = (Block*)p;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you add support for variable-sized allocations with splitting and coalescing?
- How would you verify correctness with a tiny unit test harness on a microcontroller?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of low-latency IPC, NUMA locality, and cache-coherence with careful memory ordering. It also probes design trade-offs for per-core buffering vs a centralized log.

## Key Concepts
- Per-core buffering and cross-core publication
- Sequence-numbered slots and wrap-around handling
- Memory ordering on x86-64 (relaxed/upholds with fences)
- False sharing avoidance and cacheline padding
- Validation: throughput, ordering, and backpressure under contention

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-enqueue
bool enqueue(int core_id, void* msg) {
  uint64_t pos = atomic_fetch_add(&tail[core_id], 1);
  slot_t* s = &ring[core_id][pos & (N-1)];
  if (s->seq != pos) return false; // consumer hasn't advanced
  s->payload = msg;
  s->seq = pos + 1;
  return true;
}
```

